ence behind it  but it's also a pain it goes off really  easily it oxidizes it goes brown  and it's one of the reasons why you hear  that vitamin c serums a lot of the time  you can only use them for about three  months before you have to chuck them out  ascorbic acid can also be quite  irritating because it is acidic  and so a lot of the time it's just not  really worth the bother for people  one of the ways that this gets around  this oxidation issue  is the packaging it's really innovative  i haven't actually seen this type of  packaging before  it has this sort of metered packaging  and  you can see if i squeeze it no matter  how hard you squeeze it it only comes  out one drop at a time  and so that means it's really hard for  air to go back into the product  and so yeah that's how it keeps the  vitamin c stable for so long  and i've had this for a while now i  think i've had this for maybe two months  and it is still completely clear there  is zero brown going on  a lot of other people have also said  that they've had this for a while and  they haven't seen it go brown they've  seen the bits around the thread go brown  but not the actual product coming out  it's also meant to be a spot treatment  which is why it comes out so slowly but  it does spread really easily so it's not  that hard to get enough out that you can  get it all over your face  there are other ingredients inside it  apart from the ascorbic acid there's  vitamin e  also potassium glycerizate which is  listed as an anti-acne ingredient  it's one of the active ingredients in  licorice extract and it's  anti-inflammatory there's also an  ingredient i haven't come across  before which is isopropyl methylphenol  it is meant to be antibacterial  so this seems to target two of the  factors that can help with acne so  inflammation and bacteria according to  the packaging there's also  a delivery system with this which helps  these active ingredients penetrate your  skin a bit deeper  this has a slippery oil-like texture it  sinks in pretty quickly it leaves a very  very slightly tacky finish  i found this to be one of the least  irritating ascorbic acid products i've  ever tried and it has  just the mildest of scents i've been  using this for my post-inflammatory  hyperpigmentation which i've been  getting from masks and also from trying  out a lot of sunscreens and it's been  working really well  now speaking of sunscreens sunscreens  are probably some of the most popular  asian skincare products  and that's because i think they have  such a light texture wearing sunscreen  daily has been coming in  asia for ages it's been decades and so  there are lots of lightweight asian  sunscreens that you're meant to wear  under makeup  so it's important to note that a lot of  asian sunscreens aren't  intended for like hardcore wear unless  they're specifically water resistant  they're not really designed to  protect you while you're going out and  being sweaty and moving around a lot so  if you're gardening if you're exercising  if you're going on a bike ride or going  to the beach  these are probably not going to be that  suitable they're really like commuter  sunscreens you're only meant to wear  them to and from the office and maybe  when you're heading off to go buy lunch  but there are some water resistant asian  sunscreens which have really nice  textures and these can stand up to  a bit more activity water resistant  means that they've been tested in water  so they've been applied to people the  people had to sit in water and then  after they got out after a certain  amount of time  they tested how much protection was left  but they do still say  to reapply if you sweat a lot if you get  into the water if you rub it off with a  towel  and that's because no sunscreen is like  a barrier that you never have to reapply  they do still break down and that's why  the legislation got rid of the word  waterproof because they're not really  waterproof they just hold up better  so today i'm going to be talking about  three water-resistant asian sunscreens  that i've tried and i actually really  liked  the first one is biore uv's watery  essence  i reviewed this back in 2014 on my blog  but they kept on reformulating it so it  seems like they do it every two years so  like 2015 2017 2019  and so theoretically they should be  reformulating it this year as well it's  kind of annoying because it means you  can't really rely on all reviews and you  always have to be really careful about  checking what year they're from  it can also be hard to work out which  version you're buying especially because  there is that language barrier  but i also really respect that they care  enough to keep on reformulating a  product to be better  and better even though the old product  was doing so well like in the old  versions this  is spf 50 plus and pa with four pluses  which is the highest protection level  you can get in japan  this uses a whole bunch of the newer  filters and interestingly they don't  have titanium dioxide  in a lot of asian sunscreen there is  titanium dioxide and unfortunately this  can give a white cast  what's new in the 2019 version is that  they have the micro defense formula  which they say is a world first  and what's really interesting to me is  that in their press release they  actually say that the reason they  started looking into this innovation was  because they weren't happy with their  old formula  according to them their all water-based  formulas didn't actually perform that  well  so their all water-based formulas didn't  always apply evenly so when you zoomed  in there were all these microscopic  cracks  plus they weren't sweat and shaving  resistant so some of the sunscreen  ingredients would come off quite easily  so this uses tiny capsules which are  smaller than micron size  and these capsules contain an  oil-soluble sunscreen ingredient  and they deliver it so that it applies  really evenly  so this gives higher 